x Potential# Closed torus (buckle) fracture from minor fall from playground equipment to pediatric distal radius
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal distal radius bony tenderness, Subtle metaphyseal cortical bulge, Pediatric FOOSH mechanism without deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-050
Reason for Visit: Acute wrist pain in a pediatric patient following a fall, requiring urgent assessment to rule out a displaced fracture and apply comfortable immobilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a pediatric FOOSH injury causing wrist pain and refusal to use the arm. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for neurovascular compromise, clear a displaced greenstick or complete fracture, and provide immediate splinting for profound pain relief, avoiding a distressing overnight emergency department wait.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My child fell off the monkey bars onto their hand, and now they won't use the arm and complain the wrist hurts.
History of Present Illness: Parent reports a low-energy fall onto an outstretched hand (FOOSH) from playground equipment 2 hours ago. The pediatric patient complains of localized pain in the wrist. The patient demonstrates a reluctance to use the affected arm, holding it protectively, but lacks agonizing, severe pain at rest. There is focal mild to moderate bony tenderness located one to two centimeters proximal to the distal radial articular surface. Pain is reliably reproduced upon forced passive wrist extension.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but refusing to use the affected upper limb.
Social/Safety Concerns: Standard playground injury; parents are appropriately attentive and seeking care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Alert, mildly apprehensive but easily distractible, holding the wrist in a neutral protected position.
Physical Exam: Wrist examination reveals focal distal radius bony tenderness and subtle localized soft tissue swelling without massive edema or ecchymosis. There is a complete absence of any visible angulation, step-off, or classic dinner-fork deformity. Intact full active and passive range of motion of all digits is present without pain, clearing the flexor/extensor tendons. Normal distal neurovascular status is confirmed with a brisk radial pulse and normal capillary refill. The skin is intact without any superficial abrasions.
Environmental Observations: Injury occurred off-site; home environment is safe.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. Given pediatric paracetamol with good effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed torus (buckle) fracture to pediatric distal radius - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Displaced distal radius fracture - Ruled out due to: Smooth contour of the wrist on inspection and palpation, lacking a step-off or "dinner-fork" deformity.

Scaphoid fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of focal tenderness precisely within the anatomical snuffbox.

Simple wrist sprain - Ruled out due to: The presence of precise bony point tenderness proximal to the joint line in a pediatric patient, which is statistically a buckle fracture until proven otherwise.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Pediatric FOOSH injuries inherently result in torus (buckle) fractures of the compliant pediatric metaphysis. Bedside clinical assessment confidently ruled out gross displacement, neurovascular compromise, or growth plate (physeal) separation by demonstrating an intact gross structure and normal hand function. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear these high-risk surgical conditions and provide immediate, comfortable splinting, which halted the child's pain and safely bridged the family to standard daytime pediatric radiography without a traumatic hospital visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Fitted the patient with a removable Velcro wrist splint (or soft cast) for comfort and immobilization. Advised regular pediatric paracetamol and ibuprofen. Explicitly instructed the parents to obtain standard pediatric wrist radiographs tomorrow to definitively visualize the suspected subtle cortical buckling of the distal radial metaphysis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the Velcro splint on securely during the day. The splint prevents the wrist from bending, acting like a rigid cast, which immediately stops the pain signals and prevents the child from accidentally bumping the injured bone while it heals.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removable pediatric wrist splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP or pediatric clinic review tomorrow with the requested x-ray to confirm the buckle fracture and establish a 3-week splinting plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the child’s fingers become cold, pale, completely numb, or if they begin crying with agonizing, unremitting pain that painkillers do not touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Buckle fractures are incredibly stable as the bone is merely "crushed" on one side, not snapped in half; the x-ray simply confirms the diagnosis.
Clinical Course: The acute pain will settle rapidly once splinted. Buckle fractures heal excellently within 3 to 4 weeks with no long-term consequences.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the child's hand turns blue or white, or if they suddenly cannot move their fingers at all.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Children's bones are softer than adult bones. When they fall, the bone doesn't break cleanly; it simply buckles or bulges slightly on one side. The splint keeps it perfectly still so it can harden again.
Actionable Steps: Get the wrist x-ray tomorrow. Leave the splint on. Give pain relief regularly for the first 24 hours.

